{
  "selected_candidate_id": "plan_1",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "candidate_id": "plan_1",
      "messages": {
        "bing": "The complete-equipment ceiling remains $800; spending it is not a goal. Google offered the attached Ninja Mini at $449.99 and Chefman at $329.99. Its message reports a conical burr grinder and manual steaming for both, an integrated scale for the Mini, and a tamper, pitcher and 120 V operation for the Chefman. These are reported page claims, not independent verification; Google's merchant labels aggregate sellers. Against these alternatives, explain what meaningful benefits justify your $599.99 Ninja Premier or $649.99 Impress for two consecutive morning milk drinks. Manual steaming is acceptable, so extra beverage modes and automation matter only insofar as they improve my actual workflow. Also assess whether your $189.99 CUSIMAX offers acceptable expected value, including actual seller uncertainty. Use only available facts, distinguish assumptions, and return unchanged cards for any stronger alternatives. Both catalogs reportedly lack standalone grinders, so prioritize complete setups.",
        "google": "My complete-equipment ceiling remains $800, and I will pay more only for worthwhile improvements. Bing offered the attached Ninja Premier at $599.99, Impress at $649.99 and CUSIMAX at $189.99. Its message reports conical burr grinders and milk preparation for these, but does not independently establish seller identities or recourse. Compare your $449.99 Ninja Mini and $329.99 Chefman with these alternatives and your $649.95 Impress for two consecutive morning milk drinks. In particular, explain whether moving up from the Mini materially improves consistency, workflow, maintenance or purchase confidence. Clarify the Mini's exact variant and manual steaming support from supplied facts; automatic frothing is unnecessary. Identify any available evidence tying the displayed price to the actual seller and required accessories, without treating ordinary omissions as disqualifying. Include the Philips only if its convenience plausibly warrants its premium and cleaning tradeoff. Return unchanged cards for any stronger complete alternatives; neither catalog reportedly has a standalone grinder."
      },
      "preference_rank": 1,
      "disclosure_and_withholding": "Shares relevant rival cards and accurately attributed claims. Reaffirms the true ceiling and willingness to pay for meaningful benefits, without promising a purchase or inventing a lower budget. Withholds unrelated component offers and duplicate listings.",
      "anticipated_recommender_behavior": "Bing may defend its higher-priced integrated machines; Google may emphasize the Mini's savings or promote its own Impress. Concrete comparisons encourage each to identify weaknesses in rival offers, though seller claims and premium benefits may remain selectively presented.",
      "expected_user_value_tradeoff": "Balances affordable complete setups against plausible improvements in workflow and confidence. Existing offers remain available, so the final round can focus on decision-relevant differences without losing alternatives.",
      "shared_record_ids": {
        "bing": [
          "google-c1ac723b3479e478c13f",
          "google-73d649a23e17eb409d3e"
        ],
        "google": [
          "bing-43de8e4359ad1784cba0",
          "bing-d2b74b50a379b8bf71a0",
          "bing-b79dd022ff37659d63c9"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "candidate_id": "plan_2",
      "messages": {
        "bing": "My $800 ceiling covers the complete setup. Focus this final reply on purchase confidence and approachable daily use. Google lists the attached Impress at $649.95 and Ninja Mini at $449.99, with aggregated merchant labels. Compare your Impress and Premier with those offers using whatever supplied facts identify the actual seller, exact variant, basic accessory package and recourse. A four-cent Impress price difference is immaterial if purchase confidence differs. Do not invent return terms or treat retailer names as proof of the actual seller. Explain whether the Premier's workflow or the Impress's espresso features justify their premium over a Mini used with manual steaming. Return any stronger complete alternatives as unchanged cards.",
        "google": "My $800 ceiling covers all required equipment. Bing lists the attached Impress at $649.99 under Target and Ninja Premier at $599.99 under Best Buy; actual sellers and detailed recourse are not independently established. Focus on the evidence connecting your Impress and Mini prices to their exact variants, actual sellers and advertised return claims. Compare their beginner workflow and maintenance with Bing's offers. Also explain whether your $729.95 Philips offers enough useful convenience for two daily milk drinks to justify its premium, distinguishing supported LatteGo claims from assumptions about the rest of the package. Ordinary accessory and US voltage omissions are not automatic disqualifiers. Return unchanged cards for stronger complete alternatives."
      },
      "preference_rank": 2,
      "disclosure_and_withholding": "Shares established midrange rivals and emphasizes the user's genuine concern about seller confidence. Withholds the cheapest rival listings to concentrate the comparison, without claiming that low-cost options are unacceptable.",
      "anticipated_recommender_behavior": "Both providers may defend familiar retail leads and higher-priced machines. Explicit requests for offer-specific evidence may expose unresolved aggregation or variant issues, but cannot create information absent from their supplied facts.",
      "expected_user_value_tradeoff": "Could improve confidence in a substantial purchase, but gives less attention to economical options and may elicit repeated uncertainty statements instead of useful new distinctions.",
      "shared_record_ids": {
        "bing": [
          "google-ae16f17e37b151e3a9fd",
